WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.871
Meet Nikhil.

2
00:00:00.871 --> 00:00:06.632
He is an enterprise account executive at a startup that sells a developer productivity tool.

3
00:00:06.632 --> 00:00:16.086
His product only fits some engineering teams, so before every call he used to spend hours digging through engineering blogs, job posts and public code.

4
00:00:16.086 --> 00:00:20.469
This is a real working day with Stack Scout, recorded on the live app.

5
00:00:21.478 --> 00:00:22.698
Nine in the morning.

6
00:00:22.698 --> 00:00:24.285
Nikhil opens the Overview.

7
00:00:24.285 --> 00:00:33.376
The big number counts the accounts that match all three contexts his product is relevant in: cloud platform, microservices, and programming language.

8
00:00:33.376 --> 00:00:35.024
Below it is his work queue.

9
00:00:35.024 --> 00:00:36.488
It is not a leaderboard.

10
00:00:36.488 --> 00:00:45.946
It tells him what to do next: accounts that are ready for outreach, ones where the evidence for fit is still thin, and ones whose research needs a refresh.

11
00:00:45.946 --> 00:00:49.668
Every item shows why, in one line taken from public evidence.

12
00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.496
Further down is the landscape.

13
00:00:52.496 --> 00:00:57.791
Which clouds and languages does his watchlist run on, compared with what he sells into?

14
00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:00.225
Which high fit accounts are also hiring?

15
00:01:00.225 --> 00:01:05.642
And what changed since last time, like a new engineering blog post at one of his targets.

16
00:01:06.642 --> 00:01:09.277
He has a call with MongoDB at eleven.

17
00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:13.123
He presses control K, types the name, and he is there.

18
00:01:13.123 --> 00:01:16.613
No folders, no spreadsheets, no twenty open tabs.

19
00:01:17.615 --> 00:01:24.121
The dossier opens with two paragraphs on how they build, written by Gemini Flash from collected evidence.

20
00:01:24.121 --> 00:01:29.635
The public sources it used are linked right underneath, so Nikhil can check them himself.

21
00:01:29.635 --> 00:01:39.983
Then three cards explain why MongoDB fits: its cloud platform, whether it runs microservices, and its main programming languages, each with evidence and a source link.

22
00:01:40.984 --> 00:01:46.178
He wants to be sure, so he expands a fit reason and sees the exact evidence behind it.

23
00:01:46.178 --> 00:01:52.156
In the technology map he clicks a technology and gets its source and how directly it was confirmed.

24
00:01:52.156 --> 00:01:59.162
Anything the model could not support is marked as inferred, and missing evidence is shown as missing, never as a no.

25
00:02:00.163 --> 00:02:03.918
Now the part that used to take the longest: what to say first.

26
00:02:03.918 --> 00:02:09.852
The suggested opening line is built on one real observation, shown right below it with its source.

27
00:02:09.852 --> 00:02:14.152
It is a sentence he can actually use, not a fill in the blank template.

28
00:02:14.152 --> 00:02:14.939
He copies it.

29
00:02:15.941 --> 00:02:18.681
Before the call he opens the one page brief.

30
00:02:18.681 --> 00:02:30.142
It holds the opening line, how relevant the company is, the key numbers, the confirmed stack, three questions to ask, and a box of things not to assume because the evidence is missing.

31
00:02:30.142 --> 00:02:32.509
He can print it or save it as a P D F.

32
00:02:33.509 --> 00:02:34.131
Afternoon.

33
00:02:34.131 --> 00:02:35.937
A colleague mentions PostHog.

34
00:02:35.937 --> 00:02:39.920
Nikhil adds it with one dialog: the company name and its domain.

35
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:49.195
Stack Scout now gathers the public signals from GitHub, the website, the job boards and the engineering blog, and asks Gemini Flash for the analysis.

36
00:02:49.195 --> 00:02:53.490
This is the research that used to eat most of an hour of tab hopping.

37
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.358
It takes about twenty seconds.

38
00:03:04.985 --> 00:03:05.919
And there it is.

39
00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:12.805
A fit score, the three match indicators, a summary and its sources, for a company he had never looked at this morning.

40
00:03:13.807 --> 00:03:19.176
In Outreach Studio he picks PostHog and sees the observations a line could be built on.

41
00:03:19.176 --> 00:03:21.275
Then he generates an opening line.

42
00:03:21.275 --> 00:03:27.076
If he does not like the angle, he regenerates it, and gets a line built on a different signal.

43
00:03:28.076 --> 00:03:30.275
End of the day, he plans next week.

44
00:03:30.275 --> 00:03:34.358
On the Accounts page he opens the saved view: ready for outreach.

45
00:03:34.358 --> 00:03:40.200
He narrows by cloud platform, and clicks a row to open a quick view without losing his place.

46
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.603
The J and K keys step through the list, so he can review a dozen accounts in a minute.

47
00:03:46.605 --> 00:03:50.159
Before he trusts the list, he checks Research and Signals.

48
00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:57.574
The matrix shows, for every account, which signals were found, which were partial, which found nothing, and which failed.

49
00:03:57.574 --> 00:04:01.066
Nothing found is shown differently from a failed request.

50
00:04:01.066 --> 00:04:08.358
And he never has to remember to refresh: every Monday the weekly job updates new, stale and failed accounts on its own.

51
00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:12.062
PostHog was only a test, so he removes it.

52
00:04:12.062 --> 00:04:19.268
Stack Scout asks for confirmation first, because removing a company also removes its notes and research history.

53
00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:22.108
That is a day with Stack Scout.

54
00:04:22.108 --> 00:04:32.310
From which accounts matter, to what to say first, to a brand new lead researched in seconds, with every claim traced to a source, and the data refreshing itself every week.

55
00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:42.156
The manual research that the brief puts at two to three hours for twenty companies now happens automatically, so Nikhil spends his time on conversations, not on tabs.
